35,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"I've found that our billing is based on floor area, not headcount, meaning empty desks still cost us. Also, re-running power and network costs 6,200 yen per desk every time we change the layout.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I found out that the area by the printer and door is much noisier than the window side. Also, the physio said it takes days of small adjustments to get the chairs and monitors feeling just right.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,May,,"It turns out the ""pilot"" routine takes up to nine minutes every single morning. Even worse, those unplanned shuffles we've had? They caused two weeks of total chaos and interruptions each time. That's a huge hidden cost.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,"The data is quite telling. The current plan leaves three people in the dark, which was the top complaint in the survey. Also, arrival times vary from 8:00 to 10:00, making first-come-first-served a joke.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Daisuke,,"I found that when project strands sat close, communication became much faster and we stopped needing long notes. Also, the new joiners said they learned the unwritten parts of the job just by overhearing us.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"Considering the costs, we are paying for space regardless of use, but every physical change to the layout is an added expense. We should avoid a system that requires frequent re-wiring or constant shuffling.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Minato,,"@Victoria, that makes sense. But if we don't change things, some of us will be in the noisy area. And since the chairs take days to adjust, moving every day sounds really tiring for our bodies.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,May,,"@Minato, you're spot on. Between the nine-minute morning setup and the physical toll of adjusting chairs, a ""free"" system sounds like a nightmare. We can't afford to waste that much time and energy every day.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@May, exactly. And let's not forget the badge records. A ""free"" system isn't free; it's just a reward for the early birds, leaving the rest of us to fight over the leftovers. It's fundamentally unfair.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think we should be careful. While the costs and time are important, my findings show that sitting together helps us learn and work faster. If we prioritize only efficiency, we might lose that connection.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Daisuke, I agree that collaboration is valuable. However, we must balance that with the fact that every layout change costs us money and time. A system that is too rigid might be costly, but one that is too fluid seems inefficient.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I'm worried that if we move around too much, we'll never get our chairs comfortable. If it takes days to settle in, moving every day or every few weeks sounds really stressful for our backs.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,May,,"@Minato, exactly. If we're constantly resetting our gear and losing nearly ten minutes every morning, we're just burning time. We need a layout that lets us actually settle in and focus on our work.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Daisuke, your point about collaboration is valid, but not if it means three people are stuck in a dark corner while the early birds hoard the window seats. We need a system that is transparent and fair.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Daisuke""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I understand. But if we just focus on fairness and costs, we might end up with a layout where we don't talk. I think we need a way to keep that natural learning and quick communication.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Victoria,,It seems we are caught between the need for stability—both financially and physically—and the desire for collaboration and fairness. We must find a balance that doesn't drain our budget or our energy.,"{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I just want us to be happy and comfortable. If we can find a way to keep the noise down and our chairs just right, while still being close to each other, that would be the best.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,May,,I'm with @Minato. We need to stop the bleeding of time and energy. Any option that forces us to reset our workstations daily or deal with fortnight-long shuffles is a non-starter for me. Stability is key.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"Stability is a convenient excuse for the status quo. If we just ""settle in,"" we're settling for an unfair distribution of light and noise. We need a system that is stable but explicitly fair.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Daisuke,,"I... I think we can find a way. If we have assigned seats, we can choose who sits where to help us work together, while also making sure the light and noise are shared fairly.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
